Manifest example
result = validate_manifest({
    "kind": "pico",
    "name": "lamp",
    "version": "1.0",
})

# result →
# {
#   "valid": True,
#   "errors": [],
#   "manifest": {
#     "kind": "pico",
#     "name": "lamp",
#     "version": "1.0",
#     "identifier": "open-engineering.pico.lamp"
#   }
# }

What “strong” means here
// Preferred: construction goes through validation
let id = Identifier::parse("oe.pico.lamp")?;

// Or through a checked constructor
let id = Identifier::new("open-engineering", "pico", "lamp")?;
There is no public way to build an Identifier that violates the grammar.

First domain function
/// Normalize an identifier candidate: trim whitespace and lowercase.
pub fn normalize_identifier(value: &str) -&gt; String {
    value.trim().to_lowercase()
}

Establish the independence principle
Write the native Rust test before any Python binding exists:
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn normalize_trims_and_lowercases() {
        assert_eq!(
            normalize_identifier("  OE.PICO.LAMP  "),
            "oe.pico.lamp"
        );
    }
}
Run:
cargo test --lib

A familiar starting point
def normalize_identifier(value: str) -&gt; str:
    return value.strip().lower()
This is perfectly acceptable for many situations. The course does not claim that every string transformation belongs in Rust.

The binding (simplified)
use pyo3::prelude::*;
use pyo3::types::PyModule;
use pyo3::PyResult;

#[pyfunction]
fn normalize_identifier(value: &str) -&gt; String {
    value.trim().to_lowercase()
}

#[pymodule]
fn open_engineering_kernel(m: &Bound&lt;'_, PyModule&gt;) -&gt; PyResult&lt;()&gt; {
    m.add_function(wrap_pyfunction!(normalize_identifier, m)?)?;
    Ok(())
}
(The actual course kernel uses the current stable PyO3 API; see kernel/src/lib.rs.)

Domain type (Rust)
pub struct Identifier {
    namespace: String,
    kind: String,
    name: String,
}

Python side
Those errors become typed exceptions:
from open_engineering_kernel import (
    Identifier,
    InvalidIdentifierError,
    InvalidManifestError,
    RuleEvaluationError,
)

try:
    Identifier.parse("not-valid")
except InvalidIdentifierError as e:
    print(e)   # clear message, catchable by type

